[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: false
faulty_devices: none
diagnosis_text:
I cannot reproduce the outage. End-to-end reachability between the hosts is healthy now: pc_0_0 -> pc_0_1 and pc_0_1 -> pc_0_0 both succeed with 0% loss and ~1 ms RTT. The fabric control plane is up: on leaf_router_0_0, BGP is established to both spines and the remote LAN 10.0.1.0/24 is learned and installed via 172.16.0.4 on eth0; on leaf_router_0_1, BGP is likewise up to both spines and 10.0.0.0/24 is installed via 172.16.0.6 on eth0. This confirms correct routing in both directions.

All “inter-rack” physical links are up: ethtool reports Link detected: yes on the leaf uplinks (leaf_router_0_0 eth0/eth1, leaf_router_0_1 eth0/eth1), on both spines (spine_router_0_0 eth0/1/2, spine_router_0_1 eth0/1/2), and on the super spine (super_spine_router_0 eth0/eth1). If a cable had failed overnight, we would expect a downed link and/or a BGP neighbor down; neither is observed. Additionally, the CLOS fabric would typically reconverge via the alternate spine, so a total outage would be unlikely even with a single link failure.

Root cause: No network or cable fault is present at the time of investigation; the previously reported timeouts cannot be confirmed.

Corrective fix: No action required. I recommend monitoring; if the issue recurs, capture timestamps and run ping/traceroute from the hosts at the time, and check host-level firewalls or processes that might have caused a transient block. 
=== END ===